-- title:
--   Existence of a level-one prolongation pair
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense that the image of $q$ belongs to the nonunits of $A$, let $k$ be a field of characteristic $q$ and $red : A \to k$ a ring homomorphism. Let `data` consist of a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the $q$-expansion datum, subject to the Kronecker congruence $hKr$ that the reduction of $\Phi$ modulo $q$ equals $(\mathrm{C}\,X^{q}-X)(\mathrm{C}\,X-X^{q})$, and assume the two Hecke operators $\bar\alpha$, $\bar\beta$ at level $1$ and prime $q$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ induce integral ring homomorphisms. Given a place specialisation $P$ of level $N=1$ for these data, the type of level-one prolongation pairs for $P$ is nonempty: there exist a homomorphism $\overline{red}$ from the residue field of $A$ to $k$ with $\overline{red}\circ\mathrm{residue}_A = red$, a map $\iota$ of modular function fields over the residue field into that over $k$ acting coefficientwise on Laurent series through $\overline{red}$, and two regular prolongations $R_1,R_2$ of $A$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(1\cdot q)$ with residue field the full level-one modular function field over the residue field of $A$, such that every element given by a Laurent series with coefficients in $A$ is $R_1$-integral with residue the coefficientwise reduction, $R_2$-integrality of $f$ is equivalent to $R_1$-integrality of its Fricke image with $R_2$-residue of $f$ equal to the $R_1$-residue of that image, and on the localised modular ring the composite of $R_1$-residue with $\iota$ agrees with the characteristic-$q$ reduction homomorphism `CharPReduction.modularRedLocHom`.
--
--   This is the construction, for a place specialisation at level one, of the pair of regular prolongations attached to the $q$-expansion at the cusp $\infty$ and to its transport along the Fricke involution $w_q$, with residue field exactly the modular function field in characteristic $q$ generated by $\bar j$; it is the reduction-theoretic input behind the Kronecker–Igusa description of $X_0(q)$ in characteristic $q$. The resulting datum is consumed by the statements about the Frobenius divisor law and the existence of models for $P$.
-- source:
